solution.p:
This inequality has terms with denominators {35}\text{,} {20}\text{,} and {28}\text{.} The least common multiple of these is {140}\text{.} So the first step in solving this inequality can be to multiply on each side by {140}\text{.}
\begin{aligned}
{140}\left({{\frac{4}{35}}x+{\frac{3}{20}}}\right) \amp\lt {140}\left({{\frac{5}{28}}}\right)\\
{16x+21} \amp\lt {25}\\
{16x+21-21} \amp\lt {4}\\
{16x} \amp\lt {4}\\
{x} \amp\lt {{\frac{1}{4}}}
\end{aligned}
The solution set is \{x\mid x \lt {{\frac{1}{4}}}\} using set-builder notation. In interval notation, we have {\left(-\infty ,{\frac{1}{4}}\right)}\text{.}

